\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TOblByobjControlComplSent
\item[* relevant examples:]\mbox{}\\
\begin{enumerate}
\item OPENTOSENT:\\
-- was [decided [x1 to leave at six] by x1] yesterday $\rightarrow$ -- was 
[decided [to leave at six] by x1] yesterday
\end{enumerate}
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_Controlrules
\item[* date of creation:] 14-MAR-1988 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] TOblPrepobjControlComplSent
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 25/10/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted rule to stop a false path in analysis: for modals, the control rules 
should work only in the next cycle, when the modal has been removed (He will 
persuade him to swim - he persuade him to swim).
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To interpret the subject of a complement sentences as
identical to the object of the by-phrase (and hence as the original subject of 
the higher sentence), and to
delete it. The subject must be a VAR, and is not allowed to be an EMPTY. This 
rule is meant only for NOC verbs that have as one of their interpretations 
identity of the BIGPRO in the embedded clause and x1 in the higher clause.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] One for NPVAR, one
for CNVAR. 
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] none
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:] dna
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
